1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,380
To begin with, a contained laboratory uses directional airflow to control a pathogen within

2
00:00:14,380 --> 00:00:16,740
a confined space.

3
00:00:16,740 --> 00:00:19,860
This is the primary concept of containment.

4
00:00:19,860 --> 00:00:24,520
I am now in what is known as an airlock.

5
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:32,940
So this is basically a region in the laboratory in which we have positive pressure.

6
00:00:32,940 --> 00:00:40,260
Positive pressure basically keeps any air out from this area on either side.

7
00:00:40,260 --> 00:00:46,380
So if you look on this side, if you follow the video, this is the external environment

8
00:00:46,380 --> 00:00:55,660
and we look on this side and this side is basically the area which is under containment.

9
00:00:55,660 --> 00:01:02,460
Now this area can be compared to an air curtain at a supermarket entrance.

10
00:01:02,460 --> 00:01:11,360
The air basically is a downdraft which locks in the pathogen in the containment zone.

11
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:17,380
I will now proceed to the next zone.

12
00:01:17,380 --> 00:01:22,440
So the next zone is maintained at a negative pressure.

13
00:01:22,440 --> 00:01:27,180
This negative pressure ensures that air will enter into this zone.

14
00:01:27,180 --> 00:01:32,500
However when it is filtered out for release into the environment, it will be passed through

15
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:37,020
a high efficiency particulate filter.

16
00:01:37,020 --> 00:01:44,820
This filter will filter out most of the pathogens by trapping them in a matrix.

17
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:50,300
Basically the filter has 0.22 micron size.

18
00:01:50,300 --> 00:02:03,560
So essentially all pathogens and particulates are trapped in the filter.

19
00:02:03,560 --> 00:02:07,920
Now this zone is at negative pressure.

20
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:15,660
In a negative pressure zone, the air is directed towards filters which are located in the ceiling.

21
00:02:15,660 --> 00:02:21,320
So the air flows through this, through the ceiling, through a heat power filter before

22
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:25,240
it exits into the external environment.

23
00:02:25,240 --> 00:02:31,860
This is referred to in biorisk management as an engineering control.

24
00:02:31,860 --> 00:02:38,780
Engineering controls based on directional air flow are a common component in all biological

25
00:02:38,780 --> 00:02:42,700
safety laboratories.

26
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:50,620
So as we progress through the laboratory, the directional air flow will continue to drop.

27
00:02:50,620 --> 00:02:56,660
For example, we have entered at this location and the pressure is negative here.

28
00:02:56,660 --> 00:03:02,860
So as I proceed into the suites of the laboratories areas, the pressure will keep

29
00:03:02,860 --> 00:03:07,940
dropping to a lower and lower negative pressure zone.

30
00:03:07,940 --> 00:03:14,100
This is the way in which pathogens are confined to this region of the laboratory.

31
00:03:14,100 --> 00:03:19,900
So all the work involving pathogens, such as diagnostics and experiments, takes place

32
00:03:19,900 --> 00:03:22,020
in these three suites.

33
00:03:22,020 --> 00:03:27,660
They are under negative pressure as compared to the zone at which I am standing in right

34
00:03:27,660 --> 00:03:29,300
now.

35
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:36,840
This is the way in which an engineering control is applied at this facility to contain microbes

36
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:43,900
to the respective laboratories and working areas.

37
00:03:43,900 --> 00:03:47,500
This is the basic concept of containment.

38
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:48,940
Thank you very much for watching.
